import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { DateFieldHero } from "../../demos/date-field/hero.rx"
import { DateFieldLocales } from "../../demos/date-field/locales.rx"
import { DateFieldForm } from "../../demos/date-field/form.rx"
import { DateFieldNative } from "../../demos/date-field/native.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Date Field" subtitle="A date typed or stepped one segment at a time, in the locale's order.">
    <Demo source="date-field/hero"><DateFieldHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      Import the parts and put them together. The segments come from the field's context, so a small component
      inside the root renders one <code>DateFieldSegment</code> per entry:
    </p>
    <Code file="date-field/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{"<input type=\"date\">"}</code> is in every browser, and is a segmented field already. It wasn't
      enough to build on. Tested in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Its locale is the browser's. With <code>lang="de-DE"</code> on the input, Chromium and Firefox still read
        typed digits as month, day, year in an English browser. Nothing sets the order, the separators or the
        digits for one field.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its partial input can't be read. While a segment is empty, <code>value</code> is <code>""</code> and only{" "}
        <code>validity.badInput</code> says that something was typed. Chromium and Firefox fire no{" "}
        <code>input</code> event until the date is complete.
      </li>
      <li>
        It ignores typed Arabic-Indic digits in all three engines.
      </li>
      <li>
        Only Chromium and WebKit expose its segments to CSS (<code>::-webkit-datetime-edit-*</code>). Firefox has no
        selector for them, and only Chromium has one for the picker button
        (<code>::-webkit-calendar-picker-indicator</code>).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So the segments are the library's: one <code>role="spinbutton"</code> per segment with{" "}
      <code>aria-valuenow</code>, <code>aria-valuemin</code>, <code>aria-valuemax</code>,{" "}
      <code>aria-valuetext</code> and a label, inside a <code>role="group"</code>, as in React Aria's DateField (Base
      UI has no date field). The order, separators, digits and segment names come from <code>Intl</code> for the
      field's <code>locale</code>, and the value is a <code>Temporal.PlainDate</code>. The native input stays as the
      hidden input that submits the value, so the browser's own <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and{" "}
      <code>required</code> checks are what Field reads.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each segment is a contenteditable <code>{"<span>"}</code> with <code>inputmode="numeric"</code>, chosen for
      phones: a soft keyboard opens only for an editable element, and its characters arrive as{" "}
      <code>beforeinput</code> events. In the three engines tested, a contenteditable span gets a cancellable{" "}
      <code>beforeinput</code> for each typed character, and a focusable <code>{"<span tabindex>"}</code> gets only{" "}
      <code>keydown</code>. The field cancels every edit the browser would make and applies the character to the
      segment itself. The keys that move and step, and <kbd>Backspace</kbd>, are read from <code>keydown</code>:
      WebKit sends no <code>beforeinput</code> for a <kbd>Backspace</kbd> with nothing before the caret. No phone
      keyboard was tried.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Temporal is not in Safari 27.2. The library uses the global <code>Temporal</code> and ships no polyfill: an
        app loads one (<code>temporal-polyfill/global</code>) until Safari has it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="keyboard">Typing and the keyboard</h3>
    <p>
      Digits fill the focused segment and move to the next one as soon as it can take no more: <kbd>1</kbd> in the
      month waits for a second digit, <kbd>3</kbd> moves on. <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> step a segment and wrap
      within it (an empty one starts from <code>placeholderValue</code>, today by default), <kbd>Page Up</kbd> and{" "}
      <kbd>Page Down</kbd> step further, and <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> go to a segment's ends.{" "}
      <kbd>Backspace</kbd> removes a digit, then empties the segment, then moves back. <kbd>←</kbd> and{" "}
      <kbd>→</kbd> move between segments, mirrored in right-to-left text, and every segment is a <kbd>Tab</kbd>{" "}
      stop. Pasting an ISO date (<code>2026-11-17</code>) fills every segment. When the
      month or year changes, the day is kept within the month: 31 January becomes 28 February.
    </p>

    <h3 id="labelling">Labelling it</h3>
    <p>
      Name the root as a group: <code>aria-label</code>, <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing at visible text as
      the hero does, or a <code>FieldLabel</code> inside a <code>FieldRoot</code>. Each segment is then announced
      with the field's name and its own, “Event date month”, in the locale's words.{" "}
      <code>emptyText</code> is what an empty segment says (“Empty” by default).
    </p>

    <h3 id="locales">Locales and digits</h3>
    <p>
      <code>locale</code> sets the segment order, the separators and the digits. Arabic-Indic digits are shown in{" "}
      <code>ar-EG</code>, and typed digits are taken in the locale's numerals or in ASCII. In right-to-left text,
      put <code>dir="rtl"</code> on an ancestor and <kbd>←</kbd> moves to the next segment.
    </p>
    <Demo source="date-field/locales"><DateFieldLocales /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> to hold the date yourself. <code>onValueChange</code>{" "}
      receives a <code>Temporal.PlainDate</code> when every segment is filled and the day exists, and{" "}
      <code>undefined</code> when a complete date becomes incomplete. Typing a year reports each year on the way
      (2, 20, 202, 2026), as WebKit's own date input does. Pass <code>null</code> for a controlled,
      empty field: leaving <code>value</code> out, or passing <code>undefined</code>, lets the field hold its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      While segments are only partly filled the field has no date, and keeps what was typed until the value changes
      from outside. A value set from outside, a form reset, or an optimistic save that fails replaces the partial
      edit. An owner that keeps its value without ever taking the new one (a save that isn't optimistic, refused)
      leaves the edit showing.
    </p>

    <h3 id="pickers">Inside a picker</h3>
    <p>
      A date picker puts a field and a calendar behind one value: give the field a controlled <code>value</code>{" "}
      and <code>onValueChange</code>. A part placed inside the root can read <code>use dateField</code> and call{" "}
      <code>focus()</code> to give focus back to the field (its first empty segment, or the last). A range is two
      roots, each with its own <code>aria-label</code>, inside the picker's own <code>role="group"</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A picker that keeps the Field wiring to itself passes it on to the field with <code>invalid</code> and{" "}
      <code>describedBy</code> (the ids of its description and error). Each segment then gets{" "}
      <code>aria-invalid</code>, <code>data-invalid</code> and <code>aria-describedby</code> from them, as it would
      from an enclosing <code>FieldRoot</code>; given, they win over a Field's. A date outside <code>min</code> or{" "}
      <code>max</code> is invalid either way.
    </p>

    <h3 id="validation">Bounds and validity</h3>
    <p>
      A date before <code>min</code> or after <code>max</code> is kept, not clamped: the field gets{" "}
      <code>data-invalid</code> and its segments <code>aria-invalid</code>, and the hidden input reports{" "}
      <code>rangeUnderflow</code> or <code>rangeOverflow</code>. A field with some segments filled and others not
      is invalid too, with <code>incompleteMessage</code> as its error. In a <code>FieldRoot</code>, the field
      validates as other controls do.
    </p>
    <Demo source="date-field/form"><DateFieldForm /></Demo>

    <h3 id="forms">In a form</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, a hidden{" "}
      <code>{"<input type=\"date\">"}</code> submits the ISO date (<code>2026-11-17</code>), and nothing while the
      field is incomplete. A form reset puts the default back. A Form's <code>onFormSubmit</code> and Field's{" "}
      <code>validate</code> receive the same ISO string.
    </p>

    <h3 id="saving">Saving to a server</h3>
    <p>
      The field calls <code>onValueChange</code> in a change of its own, so a handler that saves, or writes
      something a loading question reads, never holds the field: typing goes on while the request is out. Since a
      change is reported on every complete date, a field that saves as it changes saves each year typed on the way;
      save on submit, or from a picker's confirmation, where that matters. To stop changes while a save is out, pass{" "}
      <code>readOnly={"{"}pending? setting{"}"}</code>: the focused segment keeps focus and ignores keys. A field
      disabled the same way gives focus back to the segment when it is enabled again.
    </p>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling segments</h3>
    <p>
      A segment carries <code>data-type</code> (<code>month</code>, <code>day</code>, <code>year</code>, or{" "}
      <code>literal</code> for a separator) and <code>data-placeholder</code> while it is empty. The input has{" "}
      <code>data-focused</code> while a segment has focus, for the ring a native input would show. Segments set{" "}
      <code>caret-color: transparent</code> inline: each shows its whole value, and a caret has nowhere useful to
      be.
    </p>

    <h3 id="native">Styling only, without the library</h3>
    <p>
      If the browser's own locale is the right one and the value is only submitted with a form, a styled native
      input needs no JavaScript at all. Its look stops where the browser's selectors do: Firefox can't style a
      segment, and each engine draws its own picker.
    </p>
    <Demo source="date-field/native"><DateFieldNative /></Demo>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="date-field" />
  </DocsPage>
}
